Optimal Solution (Handling All 24h Edge Cases):

Key Rules:
- If hour == 0: hour becomes 12, suffix is 'AM'.
- If hour == 12: hour remains 12, suffix is 'PM'.
- If hour > 12: hour = hour - 12, suffix is 'PM'.
- If 1 <= hour < 12: hour remains as is, suffix is 'AM'.
- Maintain two-digit formatting with leading zeroes.

Python Implementation:
```python
def convert_to_12h(time_str: str) -> str:
    hh, mm = map(int, time_str.split(':'))

    if hh == 0:
        hour = 12
        period = "AM"
    elif hh == 12:
        hour = 12
        period = "PM"
    elif hh > 12:
        hour = hh - 12
        period = "PM"
    else:
        hour = hh
        period = "AM"

    return f"{hour:02d}:{mm:02d} {period}"

print(convert_to_12h("14:35")) # Output: 02:35 PM
print(convert_to_12h("00:15")) # Output: 12:15 AM
```

C++ Implementation:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <iomanip>
#include <sstream>
using namespace std;

string convertTime(const string& timeStr) {
    int hh = stoi(timeStr.substr(0, 2));
    int mm = stoi(timeStr.substr(3, 2));
    string period = "AM";

    if (hh == 0) {
        hh = 12;
    } else if (hh == 12) {
        period = "PM";
    } else if (hh > 12) {
        hh -= 12;
        period = "PM";
    }

    stringstream ss;
    ss << setfill('0') << setw(2) << hh << ":" << setw(2) << mm << " " << period;
    return ss.str();
}
```

question:
Automata Fix / Code Debugging:
Identify the logical bug in the following C function that is supposed to find the first non-repeating character in a string.

```c
char firstUniqChar(char* s) {
    int count[256] = {0};
    for (int i = 0; s[i] != '\0'; i++) {
        count[s[i]]++;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < 256; i++) {
        if (count[i] == 1) {
            return (char)i;
        }
    }
    return '$';
}
```
Why does this function return the incorrect character for input 'cognizant'?

answer_notes:
Logical Bug Identified:
The second loop iterates over the ASCII array indices from 0 to 255 (`for (int i = 0; i < 256; i++)`) rather than iterating through the original string `s` in chronological order!

Consequence:
- For input 'cognizant', all characters ('c', 'o', 'g', 'n', 'i', 'z', 'a', 't') have a frequency of 1.
- Iterating over ASCII indices 0 to 255 visits 'a' (ASCII 97) before 'c' (ASCII 99).
- As a result, the function returns 'a' instead of the true first non-repeating character 'c'!

Correct Fix:
The second loop MUST traverse the original string `s` from index 0 to length - 1:
```c
for (int i = 0; s[i] != '\0'; i++) {
    if (count[(unsigned char)s[i]] == 1) {
        return s[i];
    }
}
return '$';
```

Optimal Solution (O(N) Time, O(1) Auxiliary Space):

C++ Implementation:
```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

vector<int> subarraySum(vector<int>& arr, int target) {
    int n = arr.size();
    int left = 0;
    long long current_sum = 0;

    for (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {
        current_sum += arr[right];

        while (current_sum > target && left < right) {
            current_sum -= arr[left];
            left++;
        }

        if (current_sum == target) {
            return {left + 1, right + 1}; // 1-based indexing
        }
    }
    return {-1};
}
```
Note: If elements can be negative, standard sliding window does not work; a Hash Map with prefix sums must be used. For non-negative integers, the two-pointer sliding window is optimal with O(N) time and O(1) space.

Optimal Solution (O(N) Time, O(N) Space):

C++ Code:
```cpp
#include <string>
#include <stack>
using namespace std;

bool isValid(string s) {
    stack<char> st;
    for (char c : s) {
        if (c == '(' || c == '{' || c == '[') {
            st.push(c);
        } else {
            if (st.empty()) return false;
            char top = st.top();
            if ((c == ')' && top != '(') ||
                (c == '}' && top != '{') ||
                (c == ']' && top != '[')) {
                return false;
            }
            st.pop();
        }
    }
    return st.empty();
}
```
Time Complexity: O(N)
Space Complexity: O(N) in worst case (e.g. '((((((').

Optimal Solution (Three-Pointer Starting from End):

Key Strategy:
To avoid overwriting elements in nums1 without extra space, fill nums1 from the back (index m + n - 1) backwards!

C++ Code:
```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

void merge(vector<int>& nums1, int m, vector<int>& nums2, int n) {
    int p1 = m - 1;
    int p2 = n - 1;
    int p = m + n - 1;

    while (p1 >= 0 && p2 >= 0) {
        if (nums1[p1] > nums2[p2]) {
            nums1[p--] = nums1[p1--];
        } else {
            nums1[p--] = nums2[p2--];
        }
    }

    while (p2 >= 0) {
        nums1[p--] = nums2[p2--];
    }
}
```
Time Complexity: O(m + n)
Space Complexity: O(1).

Optimal Solution (O(N) Time using Hash Set):

Key Insight:
Only start counting consecutive sequence when `num - 1` is NOT in the set! This ensures each sequence is traversed at most twice across the entire algorithm, maintaining strict O(N) linear time.

C++ Code:
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_set>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

int longestConsecutive(vector<int>& nums) {
    unordered_set<int> numSet(nums.begin(), nums.end());
    int longestStreak = 0;

    for (int num : numSet) {
        // Check if num is the start of a sequence
        if (numSet.find(num - 1) == numSet.end()) {
            int currentNum = num;
            int currentStreak = 1;

            while (numSet.find(currentNum + 1) != numSet.end()) {
                currentNum++;
                currentStreak++;
            }
            longestStreak = max(longestStreak, currentStreak);
        }
    }
    return longestStreak;
}
```
Time Complexity: O(N)
Space Complexity: O(N) for the hash set.

